A||||10||Haagster||Is he right???||||5||
Z||000000||Haagster||09-06-2001||04:37 AM||||My 87GT died last weekend and I had it towed to the local Pontiac dealer.<P>Yesterday afternoon, I got a call from the service coordinator who explained why my car wouldn't start.  Among other things (not related to the "will not start" issue), he said that the oil pressure sending unit was defective.  Because it was sending erroneous information, the ECM shut down the fuel pump (similar to the safety feature on Ford cars).  <P>I had a bad oil pressure sending unit on my old 88 coupe, and never had it shut down the fuel pump.  Is that a reasonable diagnosis, or is he throwing a smoke screen because I am not a mechanic and wont know otherwise?  I don't want the same problem to happen again.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_4.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Wolfhound||09-06-2001||05:19 AM||||Yes. It's a resonable diagnosis, and a better one than a defective fuel pump.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000002||Haagster||09-06-2001||05:22 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wolfhound:<BR><B>Yes. It's a resonable diagnosis, and a better one than a defective fuel pump.<BR>Coy</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks.  From what he told me, it is running.  He still has to deal with a few issues before bolting it all back together (2 oil leaks and a bad water pump), but said I could pick it up this afternoon.<BR>||||reg||6
