Z||000005||87GTZ34||09-06-2001||09:23 AM||||There is no safety feature for oil pressure  on these cars.  There is fuel pump switch in the oil sender that acts as a back-up to the fuel pump relay.  If your relay was bad and the sender switch was bad, you won't get fuel.  If you don't believe this then pull the connector from the oil sender and start the car.  As long as the relay works, the car will fire right up and be none the wiser about oil pressure.  On newer PCMs, the is an oil level indicator that will shutdown the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000006||thomas_l||09-06-2001||09:30 AM||||GTZ is right. The only way that switch will kill you is if the F/P relay is also bad. The ECM has nothing to do with it except for providing a ground to the F/P relay to turn it on. Now it does have a nasty habit of shutting off fuel injectors at the autocross track  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (6k rev limit)||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||09-06-2001||09:42 AM||||He's doesn't know what he's talking about.<P>The ECM has no clue what the oil pressure even is. There is NO feed from the sender to the ECM. The ECM turns on the fuel pump through a relay. The oil pressure switch keeps it on directly (not throught the relay). <P>I'm not saying that the problem isn't in that circuit (although I think the oil pressure switch AND relay would have to be bad to keep it from starting). I'm just saying his explanation of the exact problem is  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">. This is a problem with the "service writer" concept. Sometimes things get lost in the translation from the mechanic to you.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Haagster||09-06-2001||02:37 PM||||Picked the car up this afternoon.  It worked fine until I stopped at a light (yes, it was red  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">).  Oil pressure dropped fast into the red and now will not go above 20-30 regardless of speed/RPM and the service engine light is back on.  I'll have to get the code and complain.  Looks like a phone call and another trip to the shop tomorrow.<P>More on the continuing "bad soap opera" to come...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_4.jpg"> ||||reg||9
Z||000009||Haagster||09-06-2001||05:07 PM||||And now, to add to everything else, on the way to drop the car at the Pontiac dealer, my nemesis will not idle (foot off the gas = straight to zero RPM).  It goes back inside first thing in the morning.<P>The "bad soap opera" continues...<p>[This message has been edited by Haagster (edited 09-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||87GTZ34||09-06-2001||06:18 PM||||Haagster, It sounds like one of your problems is that the fuel pump relay is toast.  The symptom is that once the car is warmed up, there is not enough oil pressure at idle to trip the (backup mechanism) oil pressure switch and the car dies.  Before you take your car to the dealer do this easy test: get in the car, turn the ignition on but don't start it.  You should hear the fuel pump turn on for approx. 2 seconds and then shut-off.  If that doesn't happen then tell the dealer you need to have the fuel pump relay checked/replaced and they should have done that the 1st time.  I had this problem w/ my first Fiero so I'm pretty sure that is one of your problems.  GL<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||1
